   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to modernizing the investment powers of the state of New York mortgage agency",
    "basePrintNo": "S7412",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7412-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§2427 & 2429-b, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7412\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to modernizing\nthe investment powers of the state of New York mortgage agency\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThe bill would add one investment vehicle to the State of New York\nMortgage Agency Mortgage Insurance Fund (\"MIF\") to permit the MIF to\ninvest in Government National Mortgage Association mortgage backed\nsecurities.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend subdivision (9) of section 2427 of\nthe SONYMA statute to expand SONYMA's MIF authority to invest in\nGovernment National Mortgage Association mortgage backed securities.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend subdivision (4) of section 2429-b of\nthe SONYMA statute to provide the MIF's mortgage insurance fund\naccount with authority to invest in Government National Mortgage\nAssociation mortgage backed securities.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nThe MIF's mission, to provide mortgage insurance in support of\naffordable housing in New York State, has never been more important.\nThe MIF generates premiums and invests them, and also invests its\nrequired reserves. The types of investments that are authorized under\nits operating statutes are similar to those found in the sections of\nthe SONYMA statute dealing with the mortgage program activities, and\nhave not been revised in decades.\n\nAs a result, the MIF's investments have typically been the most\nconservative investments available. Regardless of investment cycles,\nthe MIF has invested most of its funds in U.S. Treasury securities.\nThe earnings have been minimal, particularly during the recent\ninvestment cycle. The MIF's ability to support affordable housing\nwould be enhanced by being able to invest its funds in investments\nthat produce greater revenues. The current authorized investments,\nunchanged for years, do not suffice.\n\nThis bill would authorize the MIF to invest in Government National\nMortgage Association mortgage backed securities (\"Ginnie Mae\nsecurities\"). Ginnie Mae securities are explicitly backed by the full\nfaith and credit of the U.S. Government; and Ginnie Mae is a\nself-sustaining, profitable and wholly-owned government corporation\nlocated within the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development\n(HUD). A number of public authorities in the State are authorized to\ninvest in Ginnie Mae securities. They include the Battery Park City\nAuthority, the New York State Thruway Authority, the New York State\nEnvironmental Facilities Corporation, as well as the New York City\nHousing Development Corporation.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nIn 2013, a bill was introduced proposing a limited expansion in\ninvestment authority only with respect to MIF (A.3007-C of 2013). In\n2007, a bill proposing a limited expansion in investment authority\nonly with respect to SONYMA (S.3969) was referred to the Senate\nCorporations, Authorities and Commissions Committee.\n\n LOCAL IMPACT :\n\nThis bill would have no impact on local governments.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n",
